            {
              "id": "34079f43-f53d-36cc-b57a-89d2a904f7e1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. The `rewind` function is one such function that can potentially lead to these issues.\n\nThe `rewind` function sets the file position to the beginning of the file for the stream pointed to by stream. It's equivalent to `(void) fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET)`, but `rewind` also clears the error indicator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `rewind` function if possible. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that limit the potential for buffer overflows and other issues. For example, you can use the `fseek` function with appropriate error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rewind`, you can use `fseek` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (fp == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n\n// Instead of rewind(fp);\nif (fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `rewind` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "44d18998-57f9-35ce-9746-c82d671c8770",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. The `rewind` function is one such function that can potentially lead to these issues.\n\nThe `rewind` function sets the file position to the beginning of the file for the stream pointed to by stream. It's equivalent to `(void) fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET)`, but `rewind` also clears the error indicator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `rewind` function if possible. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that limit the potential for buffer overflows and other issues. For example, you can use the `fseek` function with appropriate error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rewind`, you can use `fseek` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (fp == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n\n// Instead of rewind(fp);\nif (fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `rewind` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
