              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions. These functions are susceptible to various types of attacks, such as collision attacks, preimage attacks, and second preimage attacks. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when a programmer uses a weak hash function like MD2.\n\nMD2 is a cryptographic hash function that is considered to be weak due to its susceptibility to collision attacks. This means that two different inputs can produce the same hash output, which can lead to security issues such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function. Hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3 are currently considered to be secure and are widely used in cryptographic applications.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD2 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you are using OpenSSL library, you can replace `MD2` with `SHA256`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest); \n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
