              "id": "48d22ca7-d936-38bd-97a2-26d164f2b817",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is greater than the size of the destination buffer. This can lead to corruption of data, crash the program, or code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by using functions that limit the amount of data copied to the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memcpy` function with a safer function like `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy_s(static_cast<void*>(nv), overlap*sizeof(T), this->v_, overlap*sizeof(T));\n```\n\nNote: `memcpy_s` is only available in C11 and later. If you are using an older version of C++, you may need to use a different function or manually check the size of the data and the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
