                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of a weak or broken cryptographic hash function. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that has known vulnerabilities, such as SHA1. SHA1 is considered weak due to known collision vulnerabilities, meaning that two different inputs can produce the same hash output, which can lead to security issues such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and do not have known collision vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA1 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you are using the OpenSSL library, you can replace the use of the `SHA1()` function with the `SHA256()` function.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA1(input, strlen(data), hash);\n\n// Recommended fix\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256(input, strlen(data), hash);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
